STEP 9 VISUAL CHECK
  Ran. Chromium was already present at ~/.cache/ms-playwright, and the run was around
  30 tool calls at the checkpoint. 3 captures (landing, demo, one landing re-capture),
  3 corrective edits, all inside the step's caps.

  Breakage found on the landing capture:
    1. The three feature drop-caps rendered invisible. The versals were gated behind an
       IntersectionObserver opacity fade, so each feature paragraph opened mid-word
       ("very update", "ut the part", "imesheets") while the versal box still held its
       layout space. Fixed by removing the opacity animation entirely; versals are now
       painted by CSS with no JS dependency. The gold section rules keep their scroll
       reveal, which also concentrates the page's motion in one device instead of two.
    2. The demo teaser on the landing cited "Filed the 14th, published the 17th" while
       the demo itself says filed 3 September, published 6 September. Corrected the
       landing to the 3rd and the 6th.

  Re-capture confirms E, P and T now paint and the dates agree across both pages. The
  demo capture needed no edits: tabs, seal rail, paywall gate with its free-read meter,
  margin notes and the sibling-pieces rail all render as intended.

  Left alone as taste rather than breakage: the seal rail's allowance line wraps to four
  short lines in its 3.6rem column, and the three commendation attributions do not sit on
